1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,965
A script that cannot decide is just a list of commands.

2
00:00:03,965 --> 00:00:05,917
Real scripts check a condition,

3
00:00:05,917 --> 00:00:06,799
choose a path,

4
00:00:06,799 --> 00:00:07,555
repeat work,

5
00:00:07,555 --> 00:00:08,500
and reuse code.

6
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:10,826
Today you learn four tools.

7
00:00:10,826 --> 00:00:11,615
If and test,

8
00:00:11,615 --> 00:00:12,799
to make decisions.

9
00:00:12,799 --> 00:00:13,128
Case,

10
00:00:13,128 --> 00:00:14,970
to pick one of many answers.

11
00:00:14,970 --> 00:00:16,286
For and while loops,

12
00:00:16,286 --> 00:00:17,272
to repeat work.

13
00:00:17,272 --> 00:00:18,193
And functions,

14
00:00:18,193 --> 00:00:20,101
to reuse a block of commands.

15
00:00:20,651 --> 00:00:23,445
Every demo is a real recording on Ubuntu.

16
00:00:25,495 --> 00:00:30,316
Scripts make decisions with if. Here is one. Type: cat age.sh.

17
00:00:30,866 --> 00:00:33,654
If the test in square brackets is true,

18
00:00:33,654 --> 00:00:35,226
the first branch runs.

19
00:00:35,226 --> 00:00:37,442
Otherwise the else branch runs.

20
00:00:37,442 --> 00:00:40,087
Dash G E means greater than or equal.

21
00:00:40,087 --> 00:00:42,231
Run it with the number twenty.

22
00:00:42,231 --> 00:00:44,233
Type: dot slash age dot S H,

23
00:00:44,233 --> 00:00:44,733
twenty.

24
00:00:45,283 --> 00:00:49,166
Adult. Now ten. Type: dot slash age dot S H, ten.

25
00:00:49,716 --> 00:00:50,152
Minor.

26
00:00:50,152 --> 00:00:53,133
The square brackets are really a command,

27
00:00:53,133 --> 00:00:53,932
named test.

28
00:00:53,932 --> 00:00:57,204
It reports true or false with an exit status.

29
00:00:57,204 --> 00:00:59,094
Type: open square bracket,

30
00:00:59,094 --> 00:00:59,457
five,

31
00:00:59,457 --> 00:01:00,112
dash G T,

32
00:01:00,112 --> 00:01:00,548
three,

33
00:01:00,548 --> 00:01:02,075
close square bracket,

34
00:01:02,075 --> 00:01:02,801
semicolon,

35
00:01:02,801 --> 00:01:04,692
echo dollar question mark.

36
00:01:05,242 --> 00:01:05,624
Zero.

37
00:01:05,624 --> 00:01:06,618
In the shell,

38
00:01:06,618 --> 00:01:07,841
zero means true.

39
00:01:07,841 --> 00:01:09,753
Now a test that is false.

40
00:01:09,753 --> 00:01:11,740
Type: open square bracket,

41
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:12,123
five,

42
00:01:12,123 --> 00:01:12,811
dash G T,

43
00:01:12,811 --> 00:01:13,193
nine,

44
00:01:13,193 --> 00:01:14,799
close square bracket,

45
00:01:14,799 --> 00:01:15,563
semicolon,

46
00:01:15,563 --> 00:01:17,551
echo dollar question mark.

47
00:01:18,101 --> 00:01:18,354
One.

48
00:01:18,354 --> 00:01:20,692
Anything other than zero means false.

49
00:01:20,692 --> 00:01:23,663
The if statement simply reads that exit status,

50
00:01:23,663 --> 00:01:24,864
and picks a branch.

51
00:01:26,914 --> 00:01:29,294
Tests come in three kinds: files,

52
00:01:29,294 --> 00:01:30,737
strings and numbers.

53
00:01:30,737 --> 00:01:31,602
Files first.

54
00:01:31,602 --> 00:01:33,478
Does notes dot text exist?

55
00:01:33,478 --> 00:01:35,786
Dash F tests for a regular file.

56
00:01:35,786 --> 00:01:37,661
Type: open square bracket,

57
00:01:37,661 --> 00:01:38,166
dash F,

58
00:01:38,166 --> 00:01:39,248
notes dot text,

59
00:01:39,248 --> 00:01:40,762
close square bracket,

60
00:01:40,762 --> 00:01:41,989
double ampersand,

61
00:01:41,989 --> 00:01:42,349
echo,

62
00:01:42,349 --> 00:01:43,287
double quote,

63
00:01:43,287 --> 00:01:44,152
file exists,

64
00:01:44,152 --> 00:01:45,090
double quote.

65
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:46,498
File exists.

66
00:01:46,498 --> 00:01:51,644
The double ampersand means: run the next part only if the test was true.

67
00:01:51,644 --> 00:01:53,716
Dash D tests for a directory.

68
00:01:53,716 --> 00:01:55,574
Type: open square bracket,

69
00:01:55,574 --> 00:01:56,075
dash D,

70
00:01:56,075 --> 00:01:56,432
docs,

71
00:01:56,432 --> 00:01:57,933
close square bracket,

72
00:01:57,933 --> 00:01:59,148
double ampersand,

73
00:01:59,148 --> 00:01:59,505
echo,

74
00:01:59,505 --> 00:02:00,435
double quote,

75
00:02:00,435 --> 00:02:01,650
directory exists,

76
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:02,579
double quote.

77
00:02:03,129 --> 00:02:04,275
Directory exists.

78
00:02:04,275 --> 00:02:09,536
The double pipe means the opposite: run the next part only if the test failed.

79
00:02:09,536 --> 00:02:11,289
Type: open square bracket,

80
00:02:11,289 --> 00:02:11,761
dash D,

81
00:02:11,761 --> 00:02:12,233
no dir,

82
00:02:12,233 --> 00:02:13,649
close square bracket,

83
00:02:13,649 --> 00:02:14,459
double pipe,

84
00:02:14,459 --> 00:02:14,796
echo,

85
00:02:14,796 --> 00:02:15,673
double quote,

86
00:02:15,673 --> 00:02:16,887
no such directory,

87
00:02:16,887 --> 00:02:17,763
double quote.

88
00:02:18,313 --> 00:02:23,775
No such directory. Next, strings. First store a name. Type: name equals Sam.

89
00:02:24,325 --> 00:02:26,516
Strings use a single equals sign,

90
00:02:26,516 --> 00:02:28,242
with a space on each side.

91
00:02:28,242 --> 00:02:29,969
Type: open square bracket,

92
00:02:29,969 --> 00:02:30,832
double quote,

93
00:02:30,832 --> 00:02:31,629
dollar name,

94
00:02:31,629 --> 00:02:32,492
double quote,

95
00:02:32,492 --> 00:02:32,957
equals,

96
00:02:32,957 --> 00:02:33,223
Sam,

97
00:02:33,223 --> 00:02:34,617
close square bracket,

98
00:02:34,617 --> 00:02:35,746
double ampersand,

99
00:02:35,746 --> 00:02:36,078
echo,

100
00:02:36,078 --> 00:02:36,941
double quote,

101
00:02:36,941 --> 00:02:37,605
same name,

102
00:02:37,605 --> 00:02:38,469
double quote.

103
00:02:39,019 --> 00:02:39,732
Same name.

104
00:02:39,732 --> 00:02:41,158
Numbers use letters,

105
00:02:41,158 --> 00:02:43,726
not symbols: dash L T for less than,

106
00:02:43,726 --> 00:02:45,580
dash G T for greater than,

107
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:46,935
dash E Q for equal.

108
00:02:46,935 --> 00:02:48,789
Type: open square bracket,

109
00:02:48,789 --> 00:02:49,217
seven,

110
00:02:49,217 --> 00:02:49,859
dash L T,

111
00:02:49,859 --> 00:02:50,144
ten,

112
00:02:50,144 --> 00:02:51,642
close square bracket,

113
00:02:51,642 --> 00:02:52,855
double ampersand,

114
00:02:52,855 --> 00:02:53,211
echo,

115
00:02:53,211 --> 00:02:54,138
double quote,

116
00:02:54,138 --> 00:02:55,137
seven is less,

117
00:02:55,137 --> 00:02:56,064
double quote.

118
00:02:56,614 --> 00:02:57,607
Seven is less.

119
00:02:57,607 --> 00:03:00,446
Now the classic trap: an empty variable,

120
00:03:00,446 --> 00:03:01,510
with no quotes.

121
00:03:01,510 --> 00:03:03,355
Type: open square bracket,

122
00:03:03,355 --> 00:03:04,277
dollar empty,

123
00:03:04,277 --> 00:03:04,774
equals,

124
00:03:04,774 --> 00:03:04,916
x,

125
00:03:04,916 --> 00:03:06,406
close square bracket.

126
00:03:06,956 --> 00:03:07,544
An error.

127
00:03:07,544 --> 00:03:09,961
The shell removed the empty variable,

128
00:03:09,961 --> 00:03:12,051
and the test lost its left side.

129
00:03:12,051 --> 00:03:13,292
Quote the variable,

130
00:03:13,292 --> 00:03:14,925
and the test stays valid.

131
00:03:14,925 --> 00:03:16,623
Type: open square bracket,

132
00:03:16,623 --> 00:03:17,472
double quote,

133
00:03:17,472 --> 00:03:18,321
dollar empty,

134
00:03:18,321 --> 00:03:19,171
double quote,

135
00:03:19,171 --> 00:03:19,628
equals,

136
00:03:19,628 --> 00:03:19,758
x,

137
00:03:19,758 --> 00:03:21,130
close square bracket,

138
00:03:21,130 --> 00:03:21,914
double pipe,

139
00:03:21,914 --> 00:03:22,241
echo,

140
00:03:22,241 --> 00:03:23,090
double quote,

141
00:03:23,090 --> 00:03:24,070
quoted is safe,

142
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:24,919
double quote.

143
00:03:25,469 --> 00:03:28,882
Quoted is safe. Always quote a variable inside a test.

144
00:03:30,932 --> 00:03:34,057
When one value has many possible answers,

145
00:03:34,057 --> 00:03:34,743
use case.

146
00:03:34,743 --> 00:03:36,877
Here is a small menu script.

147
00:03:36,877 --> 00:03:38,249
Type: cat menu.sh.

148
00:03:38,799 --> 00:03:41,776
Each pattern ends with a close parenthesis,

149
00:03:41,776 --> 00:03:44,890
and each branch ends with a double semicolon.

150
00:03:44,890 --> 00:03:47,590
The star pattern matches anything else.

151
00:03:47,590 --> 00:03:48,835
Run it with start.

152
00:03:48,835 --> 00:03:50,843
Type: dot slash menu dot S H,

153
00:03:50,843 --> 00:03:51,258
start.

154
00:03:51,808 --> 00:03:56,011
Starting. Now stop. Type: dot slash menu dot S H, stop.

155
00:03:56,561 --> 00:04:02,534
Stopping. And now a word that no pattern matches. Type: dot slash menu dot S H, jump.

156
00:04:03,084 --> 00:04:04,619
The star branch caught it,

157
00:04:04,619 --> 00:04:06,272
and printed a usage message.

158
00:04:06,272 --> 00:04:07,393
Notice dollar zero,

159
00:04:07,393 --> 00:04:09,164
the name of the script itself.

160
00:04:09,714 --> 00:04:12,570
Case is cleaner than a long chain of if statements,

161
00:04:12,570 --> 00:04:15,090
and the star branch gives you a safe default.

162
00:04:17,140 --> 00:04:21,961
Loops repeat work. The for loop walks through a list. Type: cat for dot S H.

163
00:04:22,511 --> 00:04:24,289
For each word in the list,

164
00:04:24,289 --> 00:04:25,589
the body runs once,

165
00:04:25,589 --> 00:04:27,435
with name set to that word.

166
00:04:27,435 --> 00:04:27,914
Run it.

167
00:04:27,914 --> 00:04:29,829
Type: dot slash for dot S H.

168
00:04:30,379 --> 00:04:31,227
Three words,

169
00:04:31,227 --> 00:04:32,146
three rounds.

170
00:04:32,146 --> 00:04:35,540
The list can also be files that match a pattern.

171
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:37,520
Type: for f in star dot log,

172
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:38,227
semicolon,

173
00:04:38,227 --> 00:04:38,439
do,

174
00:04:38,439 --> 00:04:38,793
echo,

175
00:04:38,793 --> 00:04:39,712
double quote,

176
00:04:39,712 --> 00:04:40,772
found dollar f,

177
00:04:40,772 --> 00:04:41,691
double quote,

178
00:04:41,691 --> 00:04:42,398
semicolon,

179
00:04:42,398 --> 00:04:42,752
done.

180
00:04:43,302 --> 00:04:45,268
One round per matching file.

181
00:04:45,268 --> 00:04:46,602
Now the while loop.

182
00:04:46,602 --> 00:04:49,340
It repeats as long as its test is true.

183
00:04:49,340 --> 00:04:51,025
Type: cat count dot S H.

184
00:04:51,575 --> 00:04:53,089
The test is dash L E,

185
00:04:53,089 --> 00:04:54,460
less than or equal.

186
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:59,941
The line with double round brackets does arithmetic: it adds one each round.

187
00:04:59,941 --> 00:05:00,735
Without it,

188
00:05:00,735 --> 00:05:02,538
the loop would never end.

189
00:05:02,538 --> 00:05:03,043
Run it.

190
00:05:03,043 --> 00:05:05,207
Type: dot slash count dot S H.

191
00:05:05,757 --> 00:05:09,725
Round one, two, three. Then the test turned false, and the loop ended.

192
00:05:10,275 --> 00:05:12,997
A loop that never ends is an infinite loop.

193
00:05:12,997 --> 00:05:14,389
If you ever start one,

194
00:05:14,389 --> 00:05:16,099
press control C to stop it.

195
00:05:18,149 --> 00:05:23,717
A function is a named block of commands that you can reuse. Type: cat func dot S H.

196
00:05:24,267 --> 00:05:26,066
You define a function once,

197
00:05:26,066 --> 00:05:26,866
with a name,

198
00:05:26,866 --> 00:05:28,599
round brackets and braces.

199
00:05:28,599 --> 00:05:30,864
Then you call it like any command,

200
00:05:30,864 --> 00:05:34,263
and the words you pass become dollar one inside it.

201
00:05:34,263 --> 00:05:35,262
Run the script.

202
00:05:35,262 --> 00:05:37,195
Type: dot slash func dot S H.

203
00:05:37,745 --> 00:05:38,119
Hello,

204
00:05:38,119 --> 00:05:38,368
Sam.

205
00:05:38,368 --> 00:05:40,488
The check function then ran twice,

206
00:05:40,488 --> 00:05:42,357
with two different file names,

207
00:05:42,357 --> 00:05:44,913
and printed a different result each time.

208
00:05:45,463 --> 00:05:47,923
Functions keep a long script readable,

209
00:05:47,923 --> 00:05:50,642
and a fix in one place applies everywhere.

210
00:05:50,642 --> 00:05:53,101
A function can also end with a status,

211
00:05:53,101 --> 00:05:54,720
using the return keyword,

212
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:56,791
and an if statement can test it.

213
00:05:57,341 --> 00:05:59,986
One rule: define a function before you call it.

214
00:06:02,036 --> 00:06:06,943
Now combine everything, in a small health check. Type: cat health dot S H.

215
00:06:07,493 --> 00:06:08,278
A function,

216
00:06:08,278 --> 00:06:08,778
a test,

217
00:06:08,778 --> 00:06:10,349
a loop and arithmetic.

218
00:06:10,349 --> 00:06:12,849
The loop walks over every argument,

219
00:06:12,849 --> 00:06:13,349
quoted,

220
00:06:13,349 --> 00:06:15,991
and the function counts the failures.

221
00:06:15,991 --> 00:06:18,348
Run it with two files that exist.

222
00:06:18,348 --> 00:06:20,562
Type: dot slash health dot S H,

223
00:06:20,562 --> 00:06:21,633
notes dot text,

224
00:06:21,633 --> 00:06:22,490
app dot log.

225
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:23,694
All good.

226
00:06:23,694 --> 00:06:26,529
Now give it a file that does not exist.

227
00:06:26,529 --> 00:06:28,783
Type: dot slash health dot S H,

228
00:06:28,783 --> 00:06:29,873
notes dot text,

229
00:06:29,873 --> 00:06:30,891
gone dot text.

230
00:06:31,441 --> 00:06:32,393
One file failed,

231
00:06:32,393 --> 00:06:34,418
and the script gave a clear count.

232
00:06:34,418 --> 00:06:36,860
A script like this can run on a schedule,

233
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:38,289
and tell you what broke.

234
00:06:38,839 --> 00:06:41,036
Decide. Choose. Repeat. Reuse.

235
00:06:43,086 --> 00:06:44,409
Four myths to clear up.

236
00:06:44,959 --> 00:06:47,937
Myth one: square brackets are special syntax.

237
00:06:47,937 --> 00:06:48,136
No.

238
00:06:48,136 --> 00:06:50,121
They are a command named test,

239
00:06:50,121 --> 00:06:52,703
so the spaces inside them are required.

240
00:06:53,253 --> 00:06:56,478
Myth two: dash E Q and equals are the same.

241
00:06:56,478 --> 00:06:56,703
No.

242
00:06:56,703 --> 00:06:58,653
Dash E Q compares numbers.

243
00:06:58,653 --> 00:07:00,229
Equals compares text.

244
00:07:00,779 --> 00:07:03,461
Myth three: a while loop stops by itself.

245
00:07:03,461 --> 00:07:03,657
No.

246
00:07:03,657 --> 00:07:06,274
It stops only when its test turns false,

247
00:07:06,274 --> 00:07:08,629
so change something inside the loop.

248
00:07:09,179 --> 00:07:11,611
Myth four: a function returns text.

249
00:07:11,611 --> 00:07:11,819
No.

250
00:07:11,819 --> 00:07:12,861
It prints text,

251
00:07:12,861 --> 00:07:14,320
and returns a status.

252
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:17,307
Capture the text with command substitution.

253
00:07:17,857 --> 00:07:20,247
Test it. Choose it. Repeat it. Reuse it.

254
00:07:22,297 --> 00:07:26,862
Here is how you check your own understanding. Six points, and you need all six.

255
00:07:27,412 --> 00:07:31,615
Write an if statement with an else branch, using a test in square brackets.

256
00:07:32,165 --> 00:07:36,090
Use file, string and number tests correctly, and quote your variables.

257
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:39,691
Write a case statement with a star branch as the default.

258
00:07:40,241 --> 00:07:42,630
Write a for loop, and a while loop that ends.

259
00:07:43,180 --> 00:07:45,719
Define a function, and call it with arguments.

260
00:07:46,269 --> 00:07:50,024
Combine them in one small script, and explain its exit status.

261
00:07:50,574 --> 00:07:52,195
To pass, you need all six.

262
00:07:54,245 --> 00:07:59,045
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine. Type every command yourself.

263
00:07:59,595 --> 00:08:04,096
One: write an if and else script that tells you whether a number is at least eighteen.

264
00:08:04,646 --> 00:08:05,734
Two: test a file,

265
00:08:05,734 --> 00:08:06,502
a directory,

266
00:08:06,502 --> 00:08:07,910
a string and a number.

267
00:08:07,910 --> 00:08:10,022
Then try the empty variable trap,

268
00:08:10,022 --> 00:08:11,558
with and without quotes.

269
00:08:12,108 --> 00:08:16,759
Three: write a case script for start and stop, with a star branch for anything else.

270
00:08:17,309 --> 00:08:19,401
Four: write a for loop over a list,

271
00:08:19,401 --> 00:08:21,374
and a for loop over star dot log.

272
00:08:21,374 --> 00:08:23,645
Then a while loop that counts to five.

273
00:08:24,195 --> 00:08:27,950
Five: write a function, and call it twice with different arguments.

274
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:30,390
Six: write a health check script,

275
00:08:30,390 --> 00:08:33,769
and run it with one file that exists and one that does not.

276
00:08:34,319 --> 00:08:37,626
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

277
00:08:53,176 --> 00:08:54,051
Quick check.

278
00:08:54,601 --> 00:08:56,286
What are the square brackets, really?

279
00:08:56,836 --> 00:08:58,991
Which case pattern catches everything else?

280
00:08:59,541 --> 00:09:01,695
What stops a while loop from running forever?

281
00:09:02,245 --> 00:09:04,293
How do you pass values into a function?

282
00:09:04,843 --> 00:09:10,561
Objective covered: MC05-O03.

283
00:09:11,111 --> 00:09:13,180
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

284
00:09:23,730 --> 00:09:28,061
Next lesson: text processing, with grep, sed, awk, cut, sort and find.

